WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.790
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.410
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.570
We're The Office ladies.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:24.740
Everybody.

10
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:26.140
Hi, everyone.

11
00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.540
Welcome to Season 2, Episode 3, Office Olympics.

12
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:31.480
Yoo-hoo!

13
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.740
Guys, if I sound grouchy today, it's because I'm in Hour 4 of a cleanse.

14
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.020
I've never done one before.

15
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.040
I think they're stupid.

16
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.920
I don't know why I'm doing it.

17
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.540
Angela, what are your thoughts on my cleanse?

18
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.120
It's a bad idea.

19
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.200
Why am I doing it?

20
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.260
I don't know.

21
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.040
I don't know.

22
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:48.360
Whatever.

23
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.860
I don't want a bunch of people writing in to us being like, you guys, cleanses are good

24
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.200
for you.

25
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.000
But you know what?

26
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.200
I just think they sound like a big pain in the ass.

27
00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:56.880
Here's the thing.

28
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.520
I was running out of the house this morning.

29
00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.580
I was late.

30
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.240
I didn't have time to make my usual breakfast of scrambled eggs and sliced strawberries.

31
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.480
I eat that every morning.

32
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.240
Oh, man.

33
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.120
I love it.

34
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.080
Didn't have time.

35
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.120
But I remembered that about eight months ago, I ordered this five-day cleanse at the

36
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.160
advice of one of my friends.

37
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.700
Who's like healthy or something?

38
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:18.540
Super healthy.

39
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.140
Looks amazing.

40
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.640
Skin is amazing.

41
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.360
And I thought, well, maybe today's the day.

42
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.520
Maybe this is a sign.

43
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.080
So I just grabbed day one of the cleanse.

44
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.940
It's like a little prepackaged.

45
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.460
You know, I had a nut bar for breakfaSt.

46
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.560
She looks like she's going into outer space, you guys.

47
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:34.980
I'm so angry.

48
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.580
She has a little box.

49
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:36.920
I'm mad.

50
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.600
You've got like, it looks like compressed food.

51
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.840
Everything's freeze-dried.

52
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.900
Compressed food in this little foil package.

53
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.640
I've already, I guess, cheated.

54
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.480
I hate that.

55
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.980
I hate that I'm shaming myself over this thing already.

56
00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.740
But I'm having coffee.

57
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.400
You're not supposed to have coffee on the cleanse.

58
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:53.700
You're supposed to have tea.

59
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.240
It's not even caffeinated tea.

60
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.300
What am I supposed to do with that?

61
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.800
What is wrong with these people?

62
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.080
That's what I'm saying.

63
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:00.340
I don't know.

64
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.000
And I already decided I think I'm going to eat a full meal for dinner.

65
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:03.700
I don't know.

66
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.680
Well, I won't tell you what I brought for lunch.

67
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:06.620
What?

68
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:08.660
Pasta?

69
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.020
I brought pasta.

70
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:10.920
Oh, my gosh.

71
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:11.580
With like a marinara.

72
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.800
I have like freeze-dried soup to eat.

73
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.380
I know.

74
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.080
I know.

75
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.300
You're going into outer space.

76
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.200
You're eating your packets.

77
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.500
Why am I even saying I'm doing a cleanse when I've already planned to eat dinner tonight?

78
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.980
I'm not doing, I'm kind of doing a cleanse.

79
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.320
I hate to break it to you.

80
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.540
You're not doing a cleanse.

81
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.420
You were rushing and you grabbed your NASA packets because you bought them and you feel

82
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.580
bad that you bought them and they've been sitting there.

83
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:35.080
Yes.

84
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.140
And also what I'm learning about you, Jenna, like I already knew this about you, but boy,

85
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.040
am I learning it now being your business partner, is that if your routine gets thrown,

86
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.880
you love routine, it sends you into this like spiral where all of a sudden you're like,

87
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.560
like if you're like, oh, I didn't get my scrambled eggs and my strawberries, do you know what

88
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:54.080
I'm going to do?

89
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:55.960
I'm going to move to Arizona.

90
00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:56.960
What?

91
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.220
It is so true.

92
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.040
I'm so thrown that I start to make really wackadoo decisions.

93
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.720
Yeah, you make some big swings.

94
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:06.160
Okay.

95
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:06.480
All right.

96
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.820
Well, let's see how it goes today.

97
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.860
Yeah, on that note.

98
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:17.640
This episode, as I said, is Office Olympics written by Mike Schur and directed by Paul Feig.

99
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.120
Yay!

100
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:19.600
Let me give a summary.

101
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.820
In this episode, Michael has to leave the office for the day to sign papers on his new

102
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:25.460
condo.

103
00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.200
He's becoming a homeowner, everyone.

104
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:34.360
Dwight goes with him, leaving Jim in charge, who decides to use the day to stage Office Olympics

105
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.580
with the employees.

106
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:42.140
Fast fact number one, Office Olympics was inspired by the King of the Hill Office Olympics.

107
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.020
Yes.

108
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:49.680
The writers on Greg's show, King of the Hill, created a real Office Olympics, and that is

109
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.960
what inspired our episode.

110
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.560
Actually, Jenna, it wasn't the writers that invented the Office Olympics for King of the

111
00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:55.780
Hill.

112
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.120
It was Tim Croston, Greg's assistant.

113
00:03:58.820 --> 00:03:59.000
Oh.

114
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.120
Tim Croston was Greg Daniels' assistant.

115
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.340
Tim is also a longtime friend of mine.

116
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:08.140
I've known him for years, and Tim and two other assistants are who set up the whole Office

117
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.320
Olympics at King of the Hill.

118
00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.080
The writers, yes, participated, Jenna.

119
00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:12.620
Okay.

120
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.380
They participated, but Tim was the mastermind.

121
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.660
He was the self-appointed commissioner.

122
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:19.400
Oh.

123
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.000
Yes.

124
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:20.720
And I-

125
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.380
He was the Jim.

126
00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.620
He was the Jim.

127
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.620
And I talked to him last night.

128
00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.440
We traded text messages, and Tim, sorry if I woke you up at 11 o'clock at night in North

129
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.620
Carolina, but thank you for responding.

130
00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.680
They had a whole opening ceremonies.

131
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.040
They had a torch.

132
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.840
They had shirts for different teaMs.

133
00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:37.300
What?

134
00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:37.760
Yeah.

135
00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:38.940
They marched through the hallways.

136
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.860
They had like a banner each team carried.

137
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.600
They had a full opening ceremonies.

138
00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.200
And you know the doves that you see at the end for our closing ceremonies?

139
00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:46.560
Yes.

140
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:47.500
They had that.

141
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.020
They had doves that went across.

142
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.020
They had several games that they had to play.

143
00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.800
It took a day and a half, this Office Olympics.

144
00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.680
What were they not doing during this day and a half?

145
00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.060
I know.

146
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.320
Well, Tim said that we wasted a full day and a half on this, and I won the award for wasting

147
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.560
the most office time.

148
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.460
So he said, so overall, I'm very proud of my time on King of the Hill.

149
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.300
He said some of the games they had were mini golf through the office.

150
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:12.620
Okay.

151
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.460
Kerplunk, which was like this game, I guess, where they had like sticks in like a tube,

152
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.720
and you drop marbles down, and then you had to twist it.

153
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:21.160
I don't know.

154
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.200
And then it went kerplunk.

155
00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.820
Tim's favorite game that they had, though, was Guess the Elevator.

156
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.720
And that made it into our episode.

157
00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:34.080
So someone went down to the lobby and then came up, and you had to guess which elevator

158
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:34.720
they came out.

159
00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.600
So he said that was his personal favorite.

160
00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.860
They also had a Twinkie eating contest, and Paul Lieberstein came in second.

161
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.160
Go Paul!

162
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.960
Because Paul was working on King of the Hill at the time as well.

163
00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.900
But he said they really got into it.

164
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.500
But they went all out, and he said one of the things that blew him away was Chuck Mangione.

165
00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.080
He is a famous musician, composer, Jenna.

166
00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.980
He actually played a recurring character on King of the Hill as himself, as the celebrity

167
00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.000
spokesperson for the Megalomart.

168
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:02.540
Yes.

169
00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.380
And he flew himself out on his own dime to play.

170
00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.620
He plays the flugelhorn and the trumpet to play at the opening ceremonies, because he,

171
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.760
in fact, actually played at the Olympics in Lake Placid, New York.

172
00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.040
This is crazy.

173
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.660
How many years did they do this?

174
00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:18.140
Do we know?

175
00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.640
This was an annual thing, right?

176
00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:20.620
I don't know that.

177
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:21.160
I don't know that.

178
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.560
I think, I mean, from what Tim texted me, they definitely went full out one year.

179
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.760
I don't know if they could sustain this, because I think they did waste a lot of time

180
00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.220
not working, and here's a random piece of trivia for you.

181
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.060
I did a voice on King of the Hill.

182
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.160
I had like a one line in the Megalomart where I said, we don't sail super squatters no more

183
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.420
on account of they're dangerous.

184
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.400
So there's my connection there.

185
00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:47.520
Can you believe that?

186
00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.320
They really went all out on King of the Hill for their Office Olympics, and that was the

187
00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.060
inspiration for Office Olympics on The Office.

188
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.780
I love all of that information.

189
00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.580
That was amazing.

190
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:05.480
Also, this episode ended up inspiring offices around the country to do their own Office

191
00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:05.820
Olympics.

192
00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.500
I mean, this got even bigger than just our TV show.

193
00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.180
Tim, you really started something.

194
00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:14.560
Thank you for that legacy, and thank you for letting me text you till almost midnight last

195
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.180
night, your time.

196
00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:22.320
Fast fact number two, writer Mike Schur makes a cameo appearance in this episode.

197
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:23.120
Oh, yeah.

198
00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.120
Appearing in a photograph as Dwight's Amish cousin, Moe's.

199
00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.440
Moe's was actually based on a character in the UPN show, Amish in the City.

200
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.500
It came out July 2004.

201
00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:42.560
Jenna, I went down the rabbit hole last night, and I watched the pilot episode of Amish in

202
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.440
the City because I wanted to see what the character of Moe's.

203
00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.840
There is a guy named Moe's.

204
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.020
First of all, the premise for the show is crazy.

205
00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:54.700
They take five Amish teens who are on their rumspringa, you know, where they can go and

206
00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:55.840
live in the world for a year.

207
00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.200
In the non-Amish world.

208
00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.360
Right, and then they decide if they want to actually take the Amish way of life, right?

209
00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:07.720
So they take five Amish teens, and they put them in a house in Los Angeles, like right

210
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:14.080
off Ventura Boulevard, overlooking the city, and they put five mainstream American, like

211
00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:14.540
teenagers.

212
00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.240
And now I think just taking five Amish kids to Los Angeles is enough.

213
00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.300
You got to throw in, there's a guy from Boston who's like, all I want to do is like, get

214
00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:24.200
laid and potty.

215
00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:24.680
Woo-hoo!

216
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.040
And you're going to put him with Moe's?

217
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.100
Moe's who like brought his wooden puzzles that he made himself.

218
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.460
I mean, there's a whole scene where he's like, do you like puzzles?

219
00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.420
And the Boston guy's like, nah, I don't like them.

220
00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.260
And he's like, oh, that's pretty much all I brought.

221
00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:41.840
Oh my gosh.

222
00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.240
This, okay, sorry.

223
00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:49.560
Your explanation of Amish in the City is making me want to watch Amish in the City.

224
00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.400
I'm very invested in Moe's.

225
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.140
I feel like I'm going to have to go back because I'm like, what happened to Moe's and

226
00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:54.600
his puzzles?

227
00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:55.380
How's he doing?

228
00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.740
Well, I guess the writers were obsessed with Amish in the City as well.

229
00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.640
And the entire first season of The Office, they would all talk about it.

230
00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:09.420
And so finally, when we got a season two, that was their way of honoring their love of Amish

231
00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:16.000
in the City, was this character of Moe's that Mike Schur, I think he thought he was just

232
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.040
appearing in a photograph.

233
00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.000
Little did he know, he would go on to play this character for nine years.

234
00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:22.780
For nine years.

235
00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:26.360
And then probably would have been in the spinoff, The Farm, had it happened.

236
00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:26.920
True.

237
00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:29.420
Of course.

238
00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.740
We also introduced another recurring character in this episode.

239
00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:33.780
It is the realtor.

240
00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.000
Yes, Carol Stills.

241
00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:41.260
Carol Stills, played by Nancy Carell, Steve Carell's wife.

242
00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:42.700
Who is hilarious.

243
00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:48.000
Nancy is hilarious, and we will talk more about her scenes with Steve as we get into this episode.

244
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:54.220
Fast fact number three, this was our first time working with director Paul Feig.

245
00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:54.780
Yay!

246
00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.140
Who is undoubtedly one of our favorite directors.

247
00:09:58.500 --> 00:09:59.740
Paul is so brilliant.

248
00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.940
And what I love is he's kind.

249
00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.440
He's just a delightful person to be around.

250
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.100
We've talked a little bit about how he and his wife Lori would host our viewing parties,

251
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.940
but this was our first time working with him.

252
00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.460
And I'll never forget when he walked on set because he was wearing a full suit and tie.

253
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.040
Oh, he is so dapper.

