00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.280
Which he wore every day.

255
00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:20.840
Every day.

256
00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.160
Every day he wore a suit.

257
00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:25.420
A lot of them were like vintage suits, so they were also super cool.

258
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:26.800
They weren't stuffy.

259
00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.680
I mean, they were like super hip.

260
00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.480
No, he and his wife Lori, they're like a smart-looking couple.

261
00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:33.080
Yeah.

262
00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:36.640
They look like people who read The New Yorker, don't they?

263
00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:37.640
Like they're super smart.

264
00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.460
They can read the long articles because those articles are super long.

265
00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:42.880
They're really smart.

266
00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.980
And their house, I remember, was like decorated, like so lovely.

267
00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:48.600
Like they had art.

268
00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:50.500
Yes, yes, art.

269
00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.180
But they're not stuffy people.

270
00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:52.740
You know what I mean?

271
00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:54.100
They're incredibly approachable.

272
00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:54.420
Yes, not flashy.

273
00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.340
Yeah, not flashy.

274
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.780
They're just really nice people.

275
00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:00.640
And Paul was one of my absolute favorite directors we ever had.

276
00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.300
You are in love with his Instagram.

277
00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:03.220
I am.

278
00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:03.600
His Instagram.

279
00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.960
She is obsessed with his Instagram.

280
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.220
I love his Instagram.

281
00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:09.240
It makes me want to travel and dress nice and have a cocktail.

282
00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:10.280
Yes.

283
00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.780
Yes, this is true.

284
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.320
Paul Feig, before this, had worked on Freaks and Geeks, which I was a huge fan of.

285
00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:23.200
And actually, Freaks and Geeks is the very first television show I ever auditioned for.

286
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.080
I wondered if you auditioned for that.

287
00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.840
That seemed like right in your wheelhouse.

288
00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.300
I did not audition for one of the regular characters.

289
00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:31.700
The show had already happened.

290
00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:35.560
But I went in for a guest appearance, I think just three lines.

291
00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:40.800
And this was because Alison Jones, who cast The Office, also cast Freaks and Geeks.

292
00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.200
This started my relationship with Alison Jones.

293
00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.300
I went in.

294
00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:45.660
I auditioned for Freaks and Geeks.

295
00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:46.860
I did not get the role.

296
00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.580
That's all right, lady.

297
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:49.460
That's all right.

298
00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:50.480
It happens sometimes.

299
00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.460
She called me back later for a TV show called Undeclared that was produced by Judd Apatow.

300
00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.500
And I did get that part, just one line.

301
00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:03.040
And then, you know, five years later, she called me to audition for The Office.

302
00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:03.980
Wow.

303
00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.220
That was all Alison Jones.

304
00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.060
But I almost worked with Paul Feig on Freaks and Geeks, but it didn't happen.

305
00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:10.320
But it was meant to be.

306
00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:11.840
Our paths would cross.

307
00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:12.620
Little did I know.

308
00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:13.940
And they did.

309
00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.240
But, you know, Paul also directed Bridesmaids, one of my all-time favorite movies.

310
00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:20.540
So, so funny.

311
00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:24.420
I remember going to that premiere because, you know, we were so excited to support Paul and

312
00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:25.260
to support Ellie.

313
00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.480
And the whole cast was just phenomenal.

314
00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.880
And I was so happy for him.

315
00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:35.140
And, you know, Paul is the type of guy, the minute he sees you, this huge smile comes across

316
00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:36.880
his face and he gives you a big hug.

317
00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:40.840
And he's just, he's just such a giving person of his self, you know?

318
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.700
And I was so happy to support him and so happy that it ended up being such a huge hit.

319
00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:50.620
I want to call your attention to Paul Feig's many books that are amazing.

320
00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:57.920
Okay, he wrote Kick Me, which is, I guess it's like his autobiography.

321
00:12:58.240 --> 00:12:59.940
For the first chapter of his life.

322
00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.720
Yes, about his adolescence.

323
00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.500
Yeah, I really feel like he could write a whole bunch more.

324
00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.760
But he's so funny.

325
00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:06.660
The book is amazing.

326
00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:13.240
And then he also wrote this book called Super Stud or How I Became a 24-Year-Old Virgin.

327
00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:15.600
It's also amazing.

328
00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:21.460
So you guys, really, if you want to dive deep on Paul Feig, I recommend these books.

329
00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:22.640
Guess what we did?

330
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.800
We called Paul Feig for this episode.

331
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.120
Who is singing?

332
00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:30.540
What has this cleanse done to me?

333
00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:31.620
It's turned me into you.

334
00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.700
It's turning you into me.

335
00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:36.160
And you're about to make some crazy life choices.

336
00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:38.620
Should we go to a bar right now?

337
00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:40.100
No, God, no.

338
00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:41.080
We should not.

339
00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:42.100
That's horrible.

340
00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:43.180
It's 11 a.m.

341
00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:43.560
also.

342
00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.960
Probably defeats the purpose of your cleanse.

343
00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:50.660
Guys, we called Paul Feig and we interviewed him about Office Olympics.

344
00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:53.300
We even included some of your questions.

345
00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.440
So after the break, we're going to talk to Paul Feig.

346
00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:58.760
We're going to ask him some questions.

347
00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.860
He's going to tell us about Office Olympics.

348
00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:01.620
I can't wait.

349
00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:02.820
All right, stay tuned.

350
00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:11.180
We're back and we're going to be talking to Paul Feig.

351
00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:12.840
Sam, dial him up.

352
00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:18.120
Hello.

353
00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:19.280
Hi, Paul.

354
00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:20.180
Hey, Paul.

355
00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:20.920
How are you?

356
00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:21.780
I'm good.

357
00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:22.320
How are you?

358
00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:23.980
Are you somewhere fancy, Paul?

359
00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.360
No, I'm in New York, actually.

360
00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:26.880
Yeah, but are you somewhere fancy?

361
00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:28.300
Well, my apartment's very fancy.

362
00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:30.660
Because I'm very fancy.

363
00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.640
Thank you so much for doing this, Paul.

364
00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:33.800
It really means a lot.

365
00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:34.900
Oh, my God.

366
00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:35.640
I'm so excited.

367
00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:37.480
I'm so happy that you guys are doing this.

368
00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.640
And I wish I could be in the studio with you.

369
00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.380
It would be so much even more fun.

370
00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:43.480
Well, you're always welcome to come back.

371
00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.500
We've got 200 episodes to talk about.

372
00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:47.900
And you directed a great deal of them.

373
00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:48.200
Oh, good.

374
00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:51.640
I'll talk on all of mine.

375
00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:53.440
We'll hit you up for that.

376
00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.800
We actually reached out to some fans to talk to you today.

377
00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.940
And there were people who had so many questions.

378
00:14:59.240 --> 00:14:59.460
Yeah.

379
00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.260
So we're going to include some of their questions with our questions.

380
00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:02.920
Very good.

381
00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:03.340
I'm excited.

382
00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:07.680
Well, to get started, Paul, how did you get involved with The Office?

383
00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:13.740
It was my great agent at the time, Renee Kurtz, who was steering me towards it.

384
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:22.740
And honestly, I was offered several years before the chance to, you know, the British office into a series.

385
00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.000
And I was like, oh, forget it.

386
00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:24.980
There's no way you can do it.

387
00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:28.280
It's too scary, you know, because it's such a great series.

388
00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.520
So, you know, I just didn't even take the bait.

389
00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:35.360
But then when Greg did it, you know, I was really, you know, amazed by that.

390
00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:43.980
And I actually, the funny thing is I was making a pilot at the same time that Greg did the pilot for The Office.

391
00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:49.100
And I remember we were both working at, like, some studio, and he was editing there.

392
00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.540
And I remember him coming over to us, to Rodney Rothman and I, whose pilot it was.

393
00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.240
And he's like, hey, come in here, come in here.

394
00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:01.500
I've got to play you a couple of different options for the opening theme song for this show that I just directed.

395
00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:08.060
And so he played a couple of different versions, including, you know, the one that the now infamous piano one.

396
00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.340
So I was definitely there right at the very beginning.

397
00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:17.580
And, you know, when the show got picked up, my agent called me, Renee, and she was just like, you know, they'd like you to direct an episode.

398
00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:24.120
And so I watched the first six that you guys had done and thought they were so hilarious.

399
00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.520
You know, as soon as I saw Diversity Day, I was just, like, completely in.

400
00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:28.680
I just thought it was so great.

401
00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:31.780
Yeah, I came to that first table read.

402
00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:35.620
I don't know if you remember, they did kind of a bunch of episodes all at once.

403
00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:39.140
And I remember you guys were reading the Dundies and all that.

404
00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:40.820
And then we read Office Olympics.

405
00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.200
And so I was in.

406
00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:47.400
Did you get to pick Office Olympics from that table read?

407
00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.100
Or was it assigned to you?

408
00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.440
Yeah, that was the one that was assigned to me.

409
00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:54.580
Because I remember having a moment going like, oh, I want to do the Dundies.

410
00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.060
That seems really fun.

411
00:16:56.360 --> 00:17:02.460
But then I just saw the potential of the Office Olympics was just going to be such a blaSt.

412
00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:06.700
I mean, it was just such a fun thing to kind of put together.

413
00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:12.060
You know, I remember just what an interesting, I don't know, what an interesting feeling it was on the set for that.

414
00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:22.520
Because, I mean, if you remember, that was sort of, you know, aside from the Olympics part of it, that was the episode that kind of started to turn everything.

415
00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:28.260
Because that's where, you know, Steve's character started to become slightly more sympathetic.

416
00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:30.260
And I don't know if you remember or not.

417
00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:32.900
We really, that kind of got found on the set.

418
00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:39.040
Because remember, we were doing the whole thing where we were presenting, you guys were presenting Michael with the, you know, with the medal.

419
00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.860
And Steve just made this decision to kind of start crying.

420
00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:45.200
You know, getting all choked up.

421
00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.000
And I remember just going like, oh, my God, Steve, that's so great.

422
00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:49.200
Like, keep going with that.

423
00:17:49.200 --> 00:18:00.100
And that was, I think that was kind of the turning point where his character took that left turn from Ricky Gervais' character and became more sort of the lovable, you know,

424
00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.240
misfit who's trying too hard for everybody to like him.

425
00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:09.140
But also, Paul, I remember in that filming of that scene that you directed all of us to be genuine.

426
00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:15.640
And you can see it on our faces that we are genuinely clapping for Michael.

427
00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:17.160
We're not mocking him.

428
00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:23.960
It was this collective moment with everyone in the office enjoying supporting one another.

429
00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.540
It was really sweet.

430
00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.620
It makes me tear up every time I watch it.

431
00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.420
I cried when Steve did that.

432
00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:30.720
Yeah.

433
00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:32.860
No, it really gets me, too.

434
00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.360
Because I think, you know what it was?

435
00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:46.840
It was just Steve's performance was so sincere that it felt, if we were kind of being snarky or, you know, laughing at him, that it made you guys such villains to do that.

436
00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.980
But it wasn't anybody's really instinct to do that.

437
00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:55.500
And so I just kind of encourage you to go along with what you were already doing, which is we were all really being affected by Steve.

438
00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:55.940
Yeah.

439
00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:56.280
Yeah.

440
00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:56.760
Yeah.

441
00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:57.960
It still gets me.

442
00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:00.680
Well, we have a fan question for you.

443
00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:03.480
Reed wrote in and said, I know.

444
00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:03.780
Oh, ooh.

445
00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:08.820
Reed says, when filming a mockumentary, do you storyboard or do you have a shot plan?

446
00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:09.560
Neither.

447
00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:13.320
What you do is go through the script.

448
00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:17.980
And I would walk around with Kelly, our AD, you know, the day before or whatever.

449
00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:20.860
And we just basically kind of go, okay, what's the bet?

450
00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:32.430
Where would we put the cameras for this scene so that we would be cross-shooting it and the cameras could where the documentary crew would actually be?

451
00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:40.620
Like, okay, you know, for me, it's like I always wanted to have depth behind us, so I never wanted to shoot in the walls whenever I could avoid it.

452
00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:46.900
And so just kind of go, okay, they would be, you know, one would be over here by this desk and then on the opposite side, something, you know,

453
00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:50.180
Matt would be over there and Randall would be on the other side.

454
00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.060
And really, that was kind of it.

455
00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:56.940
Then you'd also go, like, okay, is this going to be a spy shot where you guys don't know you're on camera?

456
00:19:57.140 --> 00:20:01.530
We'll crack in the door.

457
00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:04.190
And then that was kind of it.

458
00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:28.660
And then we would just give you comedy because just every time we shot the scene, this time, we already had.

459
00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:41.460
And then we had the confidence to keep that stuff in because I'd worked on other shows where probing or tagging something or just kind of taking a little left turn in the middle of the scene.

460
00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:47.600
But the showrunners just wouldn't put it in because they were so in love with their scripts that they just wouldn't use it.

461
00:20:47.600 --> 00:21:02.820
But the shining example of that was when we did the next episode, the Halloween episode, where we had, you know, playing the emperor and talking to Michael and kind of goading him.

462
00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:08.860
And we just found that funny angle that looked like the emperor from Star Wars where you just kind of see his face.

463
00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.120
And we just, you know, him just taunt it.

464
00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:12.220
And that went on and on.

465
00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:17.800
But Greg, I think, jettisoned some other kind of storyline just so we could keep that scene long because it was just so funny.

466
00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:27.160
And, you know, that takes a lot of confidence and sort of, you know, well, self-confidence really on the part of the showrunner to let those.

467
00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:29.890
I love that.

468
00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:30.510
Me too.

469
00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:35.350
I love hearing everything that went into how it ended up on screen.

470
00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:36.370
We were just acting.

471
00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:37.630
You did all this planning.

472
00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:38.410
I know.

473
00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.290
You put all this thought into it.

474
00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:41.330
Without you, we are nothing.

475
00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:43.190
Without you, we are nothing.

476
00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:49.050
I'm wondering if you can talk a little bit about picking the condo location.

477
00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:54.370
In this episode, because it's almost like another character it features so prominently.

478
00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:57.990
What was the criteria for picking the condo?

479
00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:08.890
You wanted something that had enough flow to it and openness that you weren't going to be stuck in little rooms, you know, with just basically walls as your background.

480
00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:13.750
And so what I really liked about it is it had that kitchen that went through to the back patio.

481
00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:21.010
So, you know, we were in the kitchen shooting Michael, kind of having his breakdown, which is still possibly, I think, the hardest I think I've ever laughed on a set.

482
00:22:21.170 --> 00:22:21.450
Yes.

483
00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:28.990
We were – when Steve was having that thing where he was having the breakdown, he kind of puts his hand on the stove and picks up the thing and then goes outside.

484
00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:30.890
Is this supposed to come off?

485
00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:32.290
And he walks out with it.

486
00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:36.910
I mean, that is like just – that's the genius of Steve.

487
00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:39.510
I literally thought I was going to die when he was doing it.

488
00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:41.370
And every time we do a take, he would do something different.

489
00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:45.730
Did we use that same condo in dinner party?

490
00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:48.930
Did we manage to keep that same place?

491
00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:52.990
It looked very familiar to me, didn't it, to you?

492
00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:53.970
Like the kitchen.

493
00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:55.430
I thought it was the condo.

494
00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:55.730
Yeah.

495
00:22:56.230 --> 00:22:56.970
That's amazing.

496
00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:04.330
Well, what was fun about that is being able to go in and turn it into Jan's place.

497
00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:04.830
Yeah.

498
00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:09.530
And that was – I mean, I don't want to jump episodes, but I mean, for dinner party,

499
00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:18.290
that was the most fun of like how do we see how Jan's woman's touch went in with kimonos on the wall and that kind of stuff.

500
00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:19.210
Oh, my gosh.

501
00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:20.330
Paul, you have to come back.

502
00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:21.150
You have to come in.

503
00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:22.870
We will have so much fun with that.

504
00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.550
I was talking to Mike Schur about this episode.

505
00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:33.650
He was the writer of this episode, and he shared with us your choice of using a wide-angle lens when filming Steve for this episode.

506
00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:35.590
Can you talk a little bit about that?

507
00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:36.130
Yeah.

508
00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:43.180
It was just – we really wanted him to feel loving because it's so much about him being overwhelmed.

509
00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:49.100
I had a very similar experience when I first met Lori, my wife, and we were going to move in together.

510
00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:52.060
And she had found this place.

511
00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:53.380
And I came over.

512
00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:54.200
It was under the guise.

513
00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:56.140
I was just going to like look at it and see what it was about.

514
00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.740
And little did I know, she kind of had the realtor there and everything.

515
00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:03.060
And like I had to sign the papers to pull the trigger.

516
00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:11.120
So suddenly I went from being like this kind of weird, lonely bachelor to like committing to moving in in front of all these people.

517
00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:12.280
And I just kind of freaked out.

518
00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:20.520
And I just remember that feeling of like suddenly the house felt very like abstract and like you're in a horror movie a little bit.

519
00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:23.900
You're having a panic attack.

520
00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:33.840
And so I wanted Steve to kind of feel that being, you know, in that house of like it's almost like, you know, I forget there's an old movie, not Obsession,

521
00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:36.120
but something where this woman's going crazy in this house.

522
00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:38.640
I just thought it would be kind of funny to give it that feel.

523
00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:41.010
That story.

524
00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:43.010
Lori, you go, Lori.

525
00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:45.110
I'm telling you.

526
00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:45.430
I know.

527
00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:45.810
Look at that.

528
00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:48.970
You know, we had celebrated our 25-year anniversary, wedding anniversary.

529
00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:55.730
Well, we got a lot of questions also about the yogurt lids.

530
00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:59.290
This was maybe the thing people are most curious about, these yogurt lids.

531
00:24:59.350 --> 00:24:59.850
Oh, my gosh.

532
00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:01.810
They went crazy about the yogurt lids, Paul.

533
00:25:02.270 --> 00:25:06.170
Mel wanted to know how many metals were made.

534
00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:12.370
Casey wants to know, were any other items considered for metals before landing on yogurt lids?

535
00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:15.970
Literally any information you can give us about these yogurt lids.

536
00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:17.210
Anything about yogurt lids.

537
00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:18.190
People want to know.

538
00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:24.550
Wow, because I remember that so distinctly, just because I remember thinking it was not at all.

539
00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:31.200
And I remember Phil, remember Phil Props coming up with those.

540
00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:37.760
And I remember him rigging that up with, you know, with the paperclips of the chain and hanging that on there.

541
00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:42.660
And I had moments of going like, I don't know if it's substantial enough because they're so light.

542
00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:45.760
But then thinking, but this is kind of brilliant.

543
00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:53.360
And so going with this, I have some weird memory, and I don't know if it's accurate or not, of like a cottage cheese lids.

544
00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:55.320
Oh, it would be larger.

545
00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:56.300
Which was a little more freaky-like and bigger.

546
00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:57.520
Yes.

547
00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:02.620
But I think we might have said they were too big, and also I think they didn't stay on the paperclip.

548
00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:06.400
So, but that's just a kind of a hazy memory.

549
00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:14.240
Steve Gordon asked, this is just kind of a fun question, which one of the freaks and geeks could work in the office?

550
00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:17.360
I think it'd have to be Lindsay.

551
00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:23.760
I think only Lindsay could really pull it together inappropriately and navigate that maelstrom.

552
00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:25.580
I hear that.

553
00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:26.720
I think that is the right choice.

554
00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:27.500
I think that is correct.

555
00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:38.980
And then, okay, Paul, we have to bring up, because Jenna and I love this about you so much, that you wear a suit every day with a tie, and that our crew loved it so much.

556
00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:43.020
On Friday, a lot of times on a set, the crew will wear Hawaiian shirts.

557
00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:43.820
It's Friday.

558
00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:47.340
But when you would work, they would all wear a tie on Friday.

559
00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:53.420
And so we were wondering, when did your origin of the daily suit start?

560
00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:57.500
It was after Freaks and Geeks, because I'll flash all the way back just briefly.

561
00:26:57.620 --> 00:26:59.660
When I was a kid, I used to wear suits and ties all the time.

562
00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:00.300
I loved that.

563
00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:02.200
I was kind of like starting at like eight years old.

564
00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:06.200
Like, I got my mom to buy me my first three-piece suit.

565
00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:11.540
Dad was so mad, because he's like, you're going to grow out of it in six months.

566
00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:15.120
And he was right, but oh, what a six months I had walking around with these suits.

567
00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.600
So it was always in me.

568
00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:24.740
But then when I got into my 20s and I became a stand-up comedian, I used to wear a suit on stage.

569
00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:29.020
And then I realized I wanted to be more, I don't know, kind of relatable to the audience.

570
00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:32.330
So I started wearing that kind of thing.

571
00:27:32.650 --> 00:27:38.050
And then when I did Freaks and Geeks, even though I didn't dress this way in high school,

572
00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:44.710
I started dressing just like kind of T-shirts and jeans, because I was trying to get in the head of like the freaks and stuff.

573
00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:46.890
And so I grew my hair really long.

574
00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.930
So you see pictures of me from then.

575
00:27:48.950 --> 00:28:06.940
My hair was super long, like down to my, and, but then, so then it was, and I was there in like jeans and a T-shirt.

576
00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:07.900
And I just didn't like it.

577
00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:13.460
I felt like really identifiable as sort of the prey, if you will, in that room.

578
00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:15.860
And so I remember going like, well, screw this.

579
00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:18.100
I'm going to go back to what I used to do and wear suits and ties.

580
00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.200
They kind of thought I was an idiot for doing it.

581
00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:24.740
But I just remember going like, I like the power structure of this better, of me in a suit, like as an adult.

582
00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:30.280
And just decided from that point on, I was like, I'm just going to wear a suit and tie all the time because I enjoy it.

583
00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:33.160
And that makes me feel like an adult and also makes me feel in charge.

584
00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:36.380
And, and I, and I look better in a suit than I do in jeans.

585
00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:37.200
So there you go.

586
00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:43.820
Well, and Paul, without your suit, I wouldn't have my favorite hashtag you do, which is drunk funkle on your Instagram.

587
00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:46.220
Thank you.

588
00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:47.820
I live for it, Paul.

589
00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:49.140
I love your Instagram so much.

590
00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:50.900
I want everyone to go follow Paul Feig.

591
00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:55.880
And, and also, Paul, I'm so excited about last Christmas.

592
00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.640
I am so excited to see it.

593
00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:58.360
I can't wait.

594
00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.060
And, you know, I loved, I loved Game of Thrones.

595
00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:04.000
So, of course, Jenna doesn't watch Game of Thrones.

596
00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:04.840
It drives me crazy.

597
00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:06.760
So, Emilia Clarke's in it.

598
00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:08.620
I'm still an Emilia Clarke fan.

599
00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:09.040
Yes.

600
00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:10.520
Even though I didn't watch Game of Thrones.

601
00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:14.340
So, this is very exciting to me because I get to see her in not Game of Thrones.

602
00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:15.780
Yes, exactly.

603
00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:19.500
And then, of course, like Emma Thompson, I cannot get enough of that woman.

604
00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:20.500
I just live for her.

605
00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:23.360
So, and Henry Golding and all of it.

606
00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:24.460
I can't wait to see it.

607
00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.400
I love, love holiday movies so much.

608
00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:29.760
So, everybody go see Last Christmas.

609
00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:30.560
Oh, thank you.

610
00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:31.120
Yay.

611
00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:31.300
Yay.

612
00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:32.160
You'll love it.

613
00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:35.060
You'll laugh, you'll cry, and it's one of the most beautiful things I've ever shot.

614
00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:36.700
It's just absolutely gorgeous to look at.

615
00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:37.940
Oh, I can't wait.

616
00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:39.160
I cannot wait to see it.

617
00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:42.540
Paul, thank you so much for taking the time to do this.

618
00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:43.260
Thank you.

619
00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:44.100
Thank you, Paul.

620
00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:45.360
Thank you.

621
00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:45.940
It's so much fun.

622
00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.440
I'm so glad you guys are doing this because the show was so special to me.

623
00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:54.820
I mean, honestly, I just, I have to pinch myself that I got to be involved in and be involved

624
00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:57.960
with so many episodes and so many kind of the bigger ones, too.

625
00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.240
And it will just always be one of my proudest, proudest moments.

626
00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:05.100
And working with you guys was just one of my great joys.

627
00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:07.340
You were just such a wonderful cast to work with.

628
00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:10.160
Every day was really fun to come in.

629
00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:12.220
And just every take was fun and different.

630
00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:15.240
And I mean, I just couldn't have had a better time.

631
00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:16.060
So thank you for that.

632
00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:17.480
And I love you guys very, very much.

633
00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:18.280
Oh, Paul.

634
00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:19.360
Thank you, Paul.

635
00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:25.960
Okay, guys, we're back.

636
00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:29.020
And now we are going to break down Office Olympics.

637
00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:29.560
Yeah.

638
00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:30.720
That was so nice talking to Paul.

639
00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:31.160
That was so great.

640
00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:31.660
All right.

641
00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.000
Let's get into this episode scene by scene.

642
00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:36.140
We start with Michael's talking head.

643
00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.460
He explains that he has come in early.

644
00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:42.900
And we learn that Ryan had to come in early to bring Michael a sausage, egg, and cheese

645
00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:43.360
biscuit.

646
00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:45.240
Ryan is so pissed.

647
00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:49.180
Angela, I noticed a little bit of Ryan sass.

648
00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:51.500
Yeah, Ryan had some sass.

649
00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:53.100
Can I get Ryan sass from you?

650
00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:53.780
Yeah.

651
00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:55.180
Do you have a card that says Ryan sass?

652
00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:58.640
I don't, because the only person that bumps me with sass is Pam.

653
00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:00.620
I knew it was targeted.

654
00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:02.800
I knew your sass was not universal.

655
00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:04.360
I knew it was personal.

656
00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:04.880
See?

657
00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:05.860
I just proved it.

658
00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:11.860
I also remember that Steve had to eat all of those egg and cheese biscuits, and he had

659
00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:13.820
a bucket under his desk because he would spit them out.

660
00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.020
Because remember, he had just gotten in this amazing shape.

661
00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:21.080
And I feel like season one, Steve might have enjoyed eating all of those.

662
00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:24.200
But season two, Steve was like, I'm not doing that to my body anymore.

663
00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:27.340
Also, though, I mean, listen, here's the thing.

664
00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:32.160
When you have to eat that much food, like the same food over and over, maybe 30 takes

665
00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.340
of that food, it turns on you.

666
00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:34.820
It does.

667
00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.820
You don't need that sausage, egg biscuit turning on you.

668
00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:38.660
It's true.

669
00:31:38.880 --> 00:31:40.060
I'd spit it out.

670
00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:40.640
All right.

671
00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:44.980
So then there's another scene that has the thing that I love so much, which is when we

672
00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:49.980
start on Michael speaking to camera, and we widen to reveal Dwight lurking over his shoulder.

673
00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:52.480
It is one of your favorite, favorite talking heads.

674
00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:56.020
And this is when Michael reveals he is buying a condo.

675
00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:56.460
Big day.

676
00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.120
Going to become a homeowner.

677
00:31:58.460 --> 00:31:59.440
Big day for him.

678
00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:05.620
And then we swoop into the main office, and did you notice this, Angela, the photo on Jim's

679
00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:05.980
desk?

680
00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:09.340
I have two note cards about these opening shots in the bullpen.

681
00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:13.900
At a minute 48 seconds, do you know what Jim's screensaver is?

682
00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:14.420
What?

683
00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.760
It's a deserted island in the middle of the ocean.

684
00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.360
I think that's a little foreshadowing to the fire.

685
00:32:20.560 --> 00:32:21.840
Which is coming next.

686
00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:22.620
Coming next.

687
00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:23.600
That desert island.

688
00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:29.540
At a minute 51, John's desk, right next to his computer, there's a photo of him holding

689
00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:30.000
a baby.

690
00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:31.140
Do you know who that is?

691
00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:31.860
It's his nephew.

692
00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:32.840
It's his nephew.

693
00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:37.080
So when we had to personalize our desk, that's one of the things John brought in.

694
00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:39.340
He was so proud to be an uncle.

695
00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:39.900
I know.

696
00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:40.460
He's adorable.

697
00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:42.440
He was so excited to put that picture on his desk.

698
00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:48.820
Then we go to the scene where Jim and Pam are throwing things into Dwight's cup.

699
00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:53.820
This is when Pam has to revive Jim because sometimes he dies of boredom.

700
00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:55.800
I remember shooting that.

701
00:32:56.120 --> 00:32:57.100
That was really fun.

702
00:32:57.400 --> 00:33:01.840
John and I got really into seeing if we really could throw stuff in the cup.

703
00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:06.460
Sometimes that happens where it's in the scene, but then in real life, you actually really

704
00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:06.900
enjoy it.

705
00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:07.760
That was a moment.

706
00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:09.900
Well, I have a little something from that scene.

707
00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:12.320
At two minutes, four seconds, boom.

708
00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.760
The boom drops in the scene.

709
00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:15.060
Really?

710
00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:15.780
The boom operator.

711
00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:16.080
Yeah.

712
00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:18.100
We got a little bit of boom and they left it in.

713
00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:20.160
As you know, it was a mockumentary.

714
00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:21.900
When that happened, they let it stay.

715
00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:25.740
They wouldn't let it ruin a take, but there's a boom in that scene.

716
00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:30.680
What about Dwight's Mozart, Butch Cassidy talking head?

717
00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:35.120
This, to me, is such a sign of Mike Schur's writing.

718
00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:42.020
I think if you go back and look at every amazing Dwight talking head, most of them were written

719
00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:42.620
by Mike Schur.

720
00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:43.940
Mike is brilliant.

721
00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:45.760
He is brilliant and he's smart.

722
00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:49.860
And I mean, I feel like I would wear a t-shirt that says Mozart and Butch Cassidy.

723
00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:55.380
You're going to get a bullet in your head thanks to Butch Cassidy if you mess with Mozart,

724
00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:56.140
everybody.

725
00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:57.640
I'm not messing with Mozart.

726
00:33:57.820 --> 00:33:58.160
No.

727
00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:00.700
Again, Michael's list of magazines.

728
00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:02.720
He lists off all his magazines.

729
00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:10.200
He asked Pam if she has forwarded, cracked an American Way magazine to his new condo.

730
00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:11.740
I just want to point that out.

731
00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:19.880
His magazines are Small Businessman, Maxim, American Way, Cracked, and then the fine arts

732
00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:21.280
aficionado.

733
00:34:21.740 --> 00:34:22.860
Yeah, the one he made up.

734
00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:23.280
Yeah.

735
